    "observation": "Typed resource catalogs discard _meta and restrict size to u32; prompt/read result envelopes have no metadata field. Existing raw transports now capture one id-matched response per serialized peer operation, and the existing notification cache uses validated raw entries."
  },
  "implementation_notes": [
    "McpResource is registered only after an enabled connected peer advertises resources or prompts. Required resource-only peers are no longer failed by an unsupported tools/list request. Configured server ids and source_root remain host-owned; remote metadata cannot overwrite them.",
    "Resources, templates and prompts share the existing per-connection notification invalidation and bounded pagination (100 pages, 2048 entries, 65536-byte cursor, 30-second aggregate timeout). List entries preserve unknown metadata and large valid resource sizes. Read/get results preserve their protocol shape and metadata.",
    "Prompt argument values are protocol strings; request URI bytes are forwarded without converting them to filesystem paths. User/assistant roles and supported content are validated. Returned prompt messages remain JSON tool output and are not inserted as conversation roles.",
    "Model-visible results exclude protocol _meta recursively. Resource payloads and prompt messages remain host data under data.mcp_result, with independent server/source_root provenance. Protocol failures retain method/error code without echoing remote message/data payloads.",
    "Cancellation, management disablement, capability advertisement and configured timeouts use the existing session pool. Resource subscriptions remain explicitly unsupported even when a server advertises them.",
    "No MCP prompt integration caller was found in the frozen Codex MCP client source; upstream resource support is not taken as proof of upstream prompts support. The explicit task22 host contract is implemented using rmcp prompt models, with model/source hashes recorded.",
    "No native media playback, hosted events, external-service acceptance, cross-platform execution or future protocol sharing is asserted."
  ],
